{
  "requirement": "Every requirement defined by a fenced `{req}` directive in a chapter under the configured mdBook source directory MUST have an evidence file named `evidence/<full-requirement-name>.json`. The full name MUST use the chapter's path-derived namespace followed by the directive name; for example, a directive named `check` in `md/requirements.md` has the full name `requirements.check`. Habeas MUST apply this rule to its own requirements in this chapter.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: the sources satisfy `requirements.ci.coverage`.**\n\n**Implementation**\n- `inspect_repository_with_progress` (`src/evidence.rs`) has an `{impl}` block for the coverage check. It runs `collect_definitions` over the book source directory taken from `book.toml` through `book_source_dir`. It then loops over every definition that is not a term and reports `evidence/{name}.json: missing for requirement at <file:line>` when the name is not in the `present` set.\n- The `present` set is filled only from top-level `evidence/<name>.json` files whose name matches a definition. A file with the wrong name, or one in a subdirectory, therefore does not satisfy coverage.\n- The full name is built in `collect_definitions` as `chapter_namespace(relative)` plus the directive name, through `full_requirement_name` (`src/lib.rs`). That function has an `{impl}` for coverage. It yields `requirements.check` for `md/requirements.md`, and `foo.bar.ready` for `md/reqs/foo/bar.md` (the leading `reqs/` is dropped).\n- `chapter_namespace` carries an `{impl}` for `requirements.ci.identifiers` only. That requirement defines the namespace rules (omit a leading `reqs/` and a final `index` or `README`), so the omission is acceptable.\n- `files_with_extension` has an `{impl}` for coverage. It discovers the chapter `.md` files and the evidence `.json` files, and it reports errors for directories it cannot read.\n- Examples also require evidence files. That is specified by `requirements.examples.evidence`, which the same function marks with `{impl}`, so it is not unspecified behaviour. Terms are excluded, which matches the requirement's wording (\"every requirement\").\n- The self-application clause (\"Habeas MUST apply this rule to its own requirements\") is covered by `tests/habeas.rs`, which runs `habeas::validate()` on the repository itself. It is marked as a test of `requirements.integration.self_test`, not of coverage.\n\n**Tests**\n- `validates_full_names_requirement_text_and_input_lines` is marked `{test}` for coverage. It writes `md/reqs/foo/bar.md` and `evidence/foo.bar.ready.json`, so it checks the namespace derivation and that a matching evidence file leaves no errors.\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` is also marked `{test}` for coverage. It declares several requirements, including one with no evidence file (`requirements.two`), and asserts that the output contains \"missing for requirement\". It also checks that orphan evidence files are reported and that evidence for the other declared requirements does not produce a missing error.\n- The cases covered are the positive, the missing-evidence and the nested-namespace ones.\n- I found no test that checks the missing-evidence message for an example, or for a requirement in an `index` chapter. The namespace rule for `index` chapters belongs to `requirements.ci.identifiers`. I do not consider either gap significant for this requirement.\n\nI found no unmarked partial implementation and no unspecified behaviour in the `{impl}` code.",
